cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Does preference alignment learn meaning or surface form?
  A controlled counterfactual benchmark
message: If you use this benchmark or these findings, please cite it.
authors:
  - family-names: Hou
    given-names: Bojian
repository-code: https://github.com/REPLACE-ME/semantic-vs-surface-preference-alignment
abstract: >-
  A controlled study of whether preference-optimized language models prefer
  responses for their meaning or their surface form. Introduces a counterfactual
  benchmark with token-length-matched syntax variants and a wording-noise floor,
  and reports that across 19 preference-optimization methods training raises
  sensitivity to meaning without reducing sensitivity to meaning-preserving
  surface changes.
